# disassembling mink's neutral object back into text that assembles to the same bytes
#
# the disassembler reads only the neutral model, so any format mink converts
# to it disassembles alike. each code section is decoded through the set's
# static decode dispatch and printed by `masc.syntax.print`, and every other
# section, and the bytes of a code section no row decodes, prints as data
# directives. symbols print as labels at their offsets, with their binding,
# visibility, type and size stated before the sections. a relocation prints
# as the symbol of the operand whose field it patches, under the specifier
# the syntax spells its kind by, or as a datum of the set's data fixups.
#
# a statement is printed only when it reads back to the same bytes: its text
# is parsed again as the assembler parses it, given the shortest form the
# assembler would give it, and encoded, and the bytes and the relocations
# must be the ones the object holds. a statement that does not is printed as
# data in the set's parcels, never as a guessed instruction, and is refused
# where the set's dialect has no one-byte data directive, as spir-v's. a set
# with a typed stream is read under a stream begun with the disassembly, to
# which every statement that reads back adds what it defines, so the
# statements after it read as the assembler read them. a relocation
# against a section symbol, or against a symbol with no name, names a label
# the disassembler states at its place, spelled by the syntax's anonymous stem.
#
# a mapping symbol, as elf's `$x` and `$d` where mink's pair of the dialect's
# format and the object's architecture states them (`mink.format.pair.Mapping`), is a fact of its section and
# place and never a label: the assembler writes them again where the code and
# data it reads back start. a range a data mark starts prints as data, and one
# a code mark starts is decoded, in any section, so llvm-mc's `$d` over data
# in `.text` reads back as that data. a code mark that names the selection
# its code was written under, as llvm-mc 23.1.1's `$xrv64i2p1_c2p0`, reads the
# code after it under that selection, which the text restates before it by the
# dialect's restatement, `.option arch, rv64i2p1_c2p0`, and the text returns
# to the object's own spelling after it.
#
# a symbol's name is written bare when it reads as one name token and quoted
# otherwise (`masc.syntax.name`), as `"a b"`. a comdat group is stated by its
# sections' `.section` group flag, signature and `comdat`, and a link-order
# section by its order flag `o` and the name of the section it is ordered
# after, as `.section .foo, "ao", @progbits, .text`. a symbol version is stated by `.symver`: a versioned name
# beside its symbol as the alias it makes, one in place of its symbol by
# `@@@`, `remove`, or an undefined name's version. a relocation no statement
# takes, a marker nothing printed rides beside, a relocation the assembler
# would resolve or one no operand or datum takes, is stated by a `.reloc` at
# its offset after the statement it patches, by the format's name of its
# kind, once a statement that takes every relocation itself is tried.
#
# a statement is read back under a scope: the object's selection, or a
# combination of the dialect's narrowings of it, as risc-v's `.option norvc`
# keeps a full-length instruction the object's selection would compress. the
# scope in force is tried first and then the others, each combination once,
# one narrowing alone before two together, up to MAX_SCOPES, and a statement
# that reads back under another is written after the directives that move to
# it, the dialect's push and pop or else its lifts, so a run of full-length
# instructions shares one scope. every section ends under the object's
# selection, or in a dialect with no restatement under the selection the text
# is under once its narrowings end.
#
# a statement that reads back widens the selection in force by what the set's
# source dialect says its instructions widen it by (`source.Source.widens`),
# as spir-v's OpCapability and OpExtension widen it, and the code after it is
# decoded and read back under the widened selection, as the assembler reads
# it. the widened selection is a basis of its own, made the first time a
# statement widens to it, its scope the one whose narrowings are those in
# force less the ones the widening lifts, and the text states nothing for it,
# as the statement itself widens what the assembler reads after it.
#
# a dialect whose text states no section but the one module its format
# builds (`dialect.Dialect.module`), as spir-v's and wasm's, has the object
# written as that module: the format builds it from the object's sections,
# in its own order, and the module is what the text is held to, as the
# object's sections are not one for one with it. a module that is one
# instruction stream (`stream.Module.stream`) is written as the statements of
# the one section the set's builder makes of its text, with no section
# directive, each held to the stream's bytes at its place. a module with text
# of its own (`stream.Module.printed`) is printed by its set and read back
# whole, and the bytes the format writes of the module it reads back to must
# be the ones it writes of the module it was printed from. the module holds
# the object's index entries as the format numbers them, and its text enters
# no section, so neither entries nor a section's kind or name are refused for
# it, and a refusal at a place of the module names its offset in the bytes
# the format writes of it (`IN_MODULE`). the neutral object holds a spir-v
# module's ids as names, its entries, so the module mink builds numbers them
# densely: a module disassembled and reassembled through the neutral object
# is an equivalent one with its ids renumbered, and the byte-exact round trip
# of a file is mink's native one.
#
# what the text cannot state is refused, naming the section and offset, with
# the output left as it was: line tables, a group that is no comdat group,
# a link-order owner no name reads back to, note records, index entries, a
# version record, and a relocation that subtracts a symbol. a nameless
# section is written as the directive that enters its kind by name, as
# `.text`, and is refused when no directive enters its kind. the first section of a name, a nameless one going
# by its directive's, is entered by it, and every later one of that name is
# opened apart by the dialect's unique number, as elf's
# `.section .text, "ax", @progbits, unique, 1`, and refused under a dialect
# that keeps no two sections of one name apart. the traits an object states are the caller's to give the assembler again, as
# they were the caller's to give, and `unwritten` reports them, as the abi row and trait rows the caller gives, so none is
# left out silently. the facts a format keys to itself, as an ELF section's type number, are the format's, which no
# source dialect states.
#
# an object file is read through mink's registered formats, its format
# sniffed from its magic and converted to the neutral model, so any format
# whose conversion mink builds disassembles.

use std.types.bool.bool;
use std.types.bool.false;
use std.types.bool.true;
use std.types.error.err;
